## Migration: `TimedExecutor` → .NET 8

### Pattern Identified: **Timeout enforcement**

Your code uses `Thread.Abort()` to kill a thread exceeding a time limit, with `Thread.ResetAbort()` in the catch block. In .NET 6+, `Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` — it simply doesn't work.

### Why It Breaks

| .NET Framework API | .NET 8 Status |
|---|---|
| `Thread.Abort()` | Throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` (`SYSLIB0006`) |
| `ThreadAbortException` | Never thrown by the runtime |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | Throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` |

### Migrated Code

```csharp
public class TimedExecutor
{
    public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
    {
        using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);

        try
        {
            var task = Task.Run(work, cts.Token);
            // Task.WaitAsync throws OperationCanceledException when the
            // token fires, even if Task.Run's body doesn't check it.
            return task.WaitAsync(cts.Token).GetAwaiter().GetResult();
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException(
                $"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }
    }
}
```

### If `work` is long-running, accept a token

The above unblocks the *caller* on timeout, but the delegate keeps running in the background. To actually **stop the work**, the delegate must cooperate:

```csharp
public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<CancellationToken, T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
{
    using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);
    var token = cts.Token;

    try
    {
        var task = Task.Run(() => work(token), token);
        return task.WaitAsync(token).GetAwaiter().GetResult();
    }
    catch (OperationCanceledException) when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)
    {
        throw new TimeoutException(
            $"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
    }
}

// Caller example:
executor.ExecuteWithTimeout(token =>
{
    foreach (var item in items)
    {
        token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested(); // cooperative checkpoint
        Process(item);
    }
    return result;
}, TimeSpan.FromSeconds(30));
```

### Key Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Explanation |
|---|---|
| **Work keeps running after "timeout"** | Unlike `Thread.Abort`, cancellation is cooperative. If the delegate ignores the token, it runs to completion in the background. Pass and check the token inside long-running work. |
| **Not disposing `CancellationTokenSource`** | Leaks internal timers. Always wrap in `using`. |
| **Swallowing `OperationCanceledException`** | Only catch it at the orchestration boundary (here, to convert to `TimeoutException`). The `when` guard ensures you only catch timeout-caused cancellations. |
| **`Thread.Sleep` inside work won't cancel** | Replace `Thread.Sleep(ms)` with `token.WaitHandle.WaitOne(ms)` or `Task.Delay(ms, token)` so the sleep is interruptible. |
| **Uncooperative third-party code** | If you cannot modify the work to check a token, the only reliable kill mechanism is `Process.Kill` on a child process. There is no in-process equivalent to `Thread.Abort` in modern .NET. |

### What Changed Conceptually

```
.NET Framework (preemptive):  runtime injects ThreadAbortException → thread dies
.NET 8 (cooperative):         you signal a token → code checks it → code exits voluntarily
```

The fundamental shift is that **no external force can stop a thread** — the running code must opt in to cancellation.
